   "langs": "Hindi and English",
   "pronoun": "",
   "from": "I am from Bahraich, born in Gulam Ali Pura Bahraich on January 1988 in a lower middle class family. I studied in Morning Star Acedemy, St. Norberts and Buddha Public School and completed my Intermediate from there. I also did preparations for CPMT, PMT, IBPS PO and Clerk. Completed my graduation in 2011 and PG in 2013 and also completed Be.d in 2018. I also used to teach in school as science and maths teacher. The community from where I come at that time was backward in terms of education, understanding their rights and standing for it. I didn't had any motivation working in this sector, but when started working in Childline my interest grew with my understanding on child rights.",
   "does": "I facilitate and provide help/assistance at organization/district level to the team in their daily work with community and in cases. Coordination and liasoning with related departments.",
   "with": "We work with marginalized/vulnerable (SC/ST, Minority) families and related departments for their upliftment.",
   "matters": "To me, Child Protection and Community participation matters the most. As I work on this aspect and have a better understanding and also achieved many milestone. For example first FIR in Child Marriage in Bahraich. Linked many families to social welfare schemes. I would like to mention one more aspect that is not mentioned above and that is behaviour change. Overall behaviour change is the core of community transformation/upliftment.",
   "moment": "In 2019, during my tenure as Centre Coordinator for Childline at DEHAT, our team received an emergency call regarding an abandoned newborn. Acting swiftly, we secured the infant and, following orders from the Child Welfare Committee (CWC), admitted the baby to the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU) at the District Hospital, Bahraich. However, we encountered severe resistance. The doctor in charge was openly hostile, barred entry to both the Childline team and CWC members during an official inspection, and misbehaved with us. Recognizing the seriousness of this obstruction, we returned to our office and briefed our leadership (Jitendra Sir), who advised immediate escalation to the District Magistrate (DM). I promptly met the DM in person, presented a formal representation, and detailed the entire incident. Taking immediate cognizance, the DM directed the Chief Medical Superintendent (CMS) of Bahraich to intervene. The CMS summoned our team right away, prompting the doctor to offer an unconditional apology. That decisive intervention became a turning point—earning DEHAT and our frontline teams profound respect and cooperation across the entire hospital network moving forward.",
   "learnt": "One thing I learned working with children that I started seeing things (Problems) with their perspective.",
   "off": "1. Travelling to natural scenic places, exploring their culture. 2. Running (Football), Swimming. 3. Gym 4. Preparing challenging dishes. 5. Try to learn new languages.",
