   "pronoun": "She/ Her",
   "from": "I have been working in the development sector for around ten years, and my journey has been shaped largely by my experiences of working with different communities and organisations. Over the years, I have worked across areas such as water and sanitation, livelihoods, community institutions, programme management, monitoring and organisational development. Working closely with communities helped me understand that development is not simply about implementing projects or achieving targets. Meaningful change happens when people understand their own needs, participate in decisions that affect their lives, and have the confidence and opportunities to claim their rights. This understanding has continued to shape the way I approach my work.",
   "does": "At DEHAT, I work closely with our teams to plan our programmes, understand how the work is progressing in the field, and identify what we can improve. I help connect what we learn from communities and field teams with our future plans, so that our programmes remain relevant to people’s needs and realities. I also support teams in documenting their work, reviewing progress and strengthening the systems we use to manage our programmes. Another important part of my role is programme budgeting and financial planning, where I help ensure that available resources are planned carefully and used for the work they are meant to support.",
   "with": "I work closely with DEHAT’s programme and field teams, who engage with children, adolescents, women, youth, farmers and community-based groups across our programme areas. Through programme planning, field visits and reviews, I also interact with community members to understand their experiences, needs and feedback. My role also involves working with partner organisations, donors and other civil society organisations to strengthen programmes and build meaningful collaborations.",
   "matters": "Community participation and leadership are the aspects of DEHAT’s work that matter most to me. I believe meaningful change happens when communities are not only part of programme activities but are able to understand their rights, identify their own priorities and take collective action. For me, our role is to create opportunities, provide information and support the process, while communities gradually take greater ownership and leadership. This is what makes change more meaningful and sustainable.",
   "moment": "One experience that has stayed with me is seeing the comfort with which women come together in DEHAT’s community spaces and share what is on their minds. In some of my interactions, I have seen women speak openly about their concerns, experiences and opinions without worrying about being judged. What stayed with me was not one particular conversation, but the sense of trust that has developed over time. It made me realise how important it is for our work to nurture relationships where people feel heard, respected and comfortable being themselves. For me, this trust is as meaningful as any measurable programme outcome.",
   "learnt": "One important learning for me has been to resist the urge to immediately offer solutions. My colleagues and people I meet in the field have helped me realise that sometimes it is more important to simply listen, reflect and ask the right questions. Often, people already have their own understanding and possible ways forward; what is needed is the space to think, speak and explore them.",
   "off": "Outside of work, one of my favourite things is simply spending time with my daughter—playing with her, listening to her stories and seeing the world through her curiosity. Those ordinary moments of just being with her are something I truly enjoy.",
   "stem": "मेरे लिए विकास का अर्थ है...",
   "finish": "For me, development means creating spaces where people can voice their thoughts, recognise their own strengths, make informed choices, and shape the changes they want to see in their lives and communities.",
